test(eval): add corpus integrity gate and tier-B edge-case documents

eval/corpus/ now has the manifest contract, the verification gate and the
synthetic half of the corpus. Tier A is deliberately absent (PROPOSAL P-1 in
the remediation log): sourcing real bilingual documents with truthful licence
and URL provenance is not something I can do, and the existing
backend/documents/ tree holds third-party copyrighted material that must not
be committed.

verify.py re-hashes every manifest entry and hard-fails on a mismatch, a
missing file, or an untracked extra file in the corpus tree - an unlisted
document would be ingested and silently move every retrieval metric. Proven
in both directions: clean tree passes (12 documents, exit 0); appending one
byte to b01 fails with a sha256 mismatch and exit 1.

Tier B covers every extractor path: txt, md, csv, xlsx (multi-sheet), docx
(headings plus an in-position table), pptx, pdf with a text layer, and an
image-only pdf that forces the OCR fallback, plus three topical distractors
containing no answers.

b04-wide-row.csv is the important one: a ~13k-character single CSV row.
dynamic_rows_per_chunk clamps with max(1, ...) at helper.py:18-21, so it
bypasses table_chunk_char_budget, and the 2048-token embedding window then
drops its tail silently. It makes that defect measurable.

Note: the generator is NOT byte-deterministic for .xlsx and .pdf. I claimed
it was, then disproved it by running it twice and diffing; openpyxl and
PyMuPDF embed time-varying state that survived pinning docProps timestamps
and the PDF CreationDate, ModDate and ID. The docstring now records the
measured behaviour. Baseline integrity comes from pinning the committed
bytes, which the manifest and verify.py do, not from reproducible
regeneration.

Refs: findings #19, #24; docs/REMEDIATION_LOG.md Section 1
